WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.715
Federal lobbying pipeline.

00:00:01.715 --> 00:00:08.227
OSINT analysis of five hundred and seventy-nine registered lobbying communications across twelve federal departments.

00:00:08.227 --> 00:00:18.284
This investigation maps the institutional influence chain from lobbying registration to policy outcome, including thirty-eight Members of Parliament engaged and six sponsored travel events.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:22.215
All data sourced from the Office of the Commissioner of Lobbying of Canada.

00:00:22.215 --> 00:00:24.136
How federal lobbying works.

00:00:24.136 --> 00:00:30.352
The Lobbying Act requires organizations communicating with federal public office holders to register and report activities.

00:00:30.352 --> 00:00:37.681
The threshold changed in January twenty twenty-six to eight or more hours in a four-week period, down from twenty percent of duties.

00:00:37.681 --> 00:00:43.647
The Commissioner of Lobbying maintains a public registry but has limited investigative and enforcement power.

00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:45.681
Departments most frequently lobbied.

00:00:45.681 --> 00:00:53.113
The OSINT analysis maps which federal departments receive the most lobbying attention, identifying patterns of institutional targeting.

00:00:53.113 --> 00:00:59.215
Key departments include the Prime Minister's Office, Heritage, Justice, Public Safety, and Finance.

00:00:59.215 --> 00:01:01.795
Lobbying communication to policy outcome.

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:07.715
This section traces the direct pipeline from registered lobbying communications to enacted legislation.

00:01:07.715 --> 00:01:15.920
The Online Harms Act, the Foreign Influence Registry, and immigration policy changes all show documented lobbying activity preceding the policy output.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.238
The pattern: lobby, legislate, benefit.

00:01:19.238 --> 00:01:21.295
Sponsored travel disclosures.

00:01:21.295 --> 00:01:27.863
Under the Conflict of Interest Act, Members of Parliament must disclose sponsored travel paid by outside organizations.

00:01:27.863 --> 00:01:37.818
Six documented sponsored travel events provided direct access to lawmakers in informal settings — trips that correlate with subsequent policy actions favourable to the sponsoring organizations.
